WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.800 --> 00:00:01.533
Seaborn.

2
00:00:01.833 --> 00:00:04.933
The speed tool for exploring data before you model it.

3
00:00:06.133 --> 00:00:09.433
Seaborn is statistical plotting on top of matplotlib.

4
00:00:09.733 --> 00:00:12.633
Heatmap draws annotated correlation matrices.

5
00:00:12.933 --> 00:00:15.600
Pairplot plots every feature against every other.

6
00:00:15.900 --> 00:00:18.933
Histograms and density plots show distributions.

7
00:00:19.233 --> 00:00:21.167
And box plots compare groups.

8
00:00:21.167 --> 00:00:25.033
Each is one line from a DataFrame, with sensible defaults.

9
00:00:26.233 --> 00:00:27.500
Here's the key idea.

10
00:00:27.800 --> 00:00:29.900
At the bottom, the matplotlib figure.

11
00:00:30.200 --> 00:00:36.167
Axes-level functions, like heatmap and histplot, draw onto a matplotlib Axes and return it.

12
00:00:36.467 --> 00:00:41.072
So matplotlib still applies: pass ax, tweak titles, save with savefig.

13
00:00:41.072 --> 00:00:46.467
Figure-level ones, like pairplot, build their own figure and return a grid object.

14
00:00:47.667 --> 00:00:49.100
Here's what a heatmap shows.

15
00:00:49.400 --> 00:00:54.367
Each cell is the correlation between two features, and the diagonal is always one.

16
00:00:54.667 --> 00:00:59.052
Transit depth and estimated radius correlate at zero point nine six.

17
00:00:59.052 --> 00:01:00.600
They're near-duplicates.

18
00:01:00.900 --> 00:01:04.633
Period barely relates to either, so it carries separate information.

19
00:01:05.833 --> 00:01:07.100
In code, it's short.

20
00:01:07.400 --> 00:01:09.300
Select the numeric feature columns.

21
00:01:09.600 --> 00:01:15.333
One heatmap call draws their correlations, annotated, centered at zero, onto our axes.

22
00:01:15.633 --> 00:01:19.167
Then we set a title and save, just like any matplotlib plot.

23
00:01:20.367 --> 00:01:21.333
A real case.

24
00:01:21.633 --> 00:01:26.567
Before training the exoplanet classifier, the correlation heatmap is your first slide.

25
00:01:26.867 --> 00:01:31.100
It shows transit depth and radius estimate at zero point nine six.

26
00:01:31.400 --> 00:01:32.466
Drop one of them.

27
00:01:32.466 --> 00:01:40.433
One line of seaborn surfaced a modeling decision that would otherwise appear weeks later as mysterious coefficient instability.

28
00:01:41.633 --> 00:01:42.475
Let's recap.

29
00:01:42.475 --> 00:01:45.633
Seaborn is statistical sugar over matplotlib.

30
00:01:45.933 --> 00:01:48.533
One line per plot, straight from a DataFrame.

31
00:01:48.833 --> 00:01:52.993
Axes-level plots return an Axes, so matplotlib still applies.

32
00:01:52.993 --> 00:01:56.267
Figure-level ones, like pairplot, return a grid.

33
00:01:56.567 --> 00:01:59.192
And it's your speed tool for exploration.

34
00:01:59.192 --> 00:02:00.600
Next up: scikit-learn.
